 "title": "n squared vs n log n",
 "sources": [
  "Arithmetic only: n = 1,000,000; n^2 = 10^12; log2(1,000,000) is about 20, so n log n is about 2 x 10^7; ratio 10^12 / (2 x 10^7) = 50,000. At an example speed of 10^9 simple steps per second: 10^12 steps = 1,000 s (about 17 minutes) and 2 x 10^7 steps = 0.02 s (20 ms). At n = 10: n^2 = 100, n log2 n is about 33.",
  "The 'billion steps per second' speed is an example to make the gap concrete, shown on screen as an example."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Sorting a million items. One algorithm takes n squared steps, another takes n log n. How big is the gap? Guess.",
   "caps": "*1 MILLION ITEMS | *N² VS N LOG N | HOW BIG A *GAP?"
  },
  {
   "text": "n squared is a million times a million. That's a trillion steps.",
   "caps": "*N² | A *TRILLION STEPS"
  },
  {
   "text": "n log n is a million times about twenty. That's twenty million.",
   "caps": "*N LOG N | *20 MILLION"
  },
  {
   "text": "That's fifty thousand times fewer steps.",
   "caps": "*50,000X | *FEWER STEPS"
  },
  {
   "text": "At a billion steps a second, that's twenty milliseconds versus about seventeen minutes.",
   "caps": "*20 MS | VERSUS | *17 MINUTES"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: with ten items, the gap is tiny, so either is fine.",
   "caps": "THE *CATCH | AT *10 ITEMS | GAP IS *TINY"
  },
  {
   "text": "Big O only matters as the input grows, which is exactly when your app gets popular.",
   "caps": "MATTERS AS INPUT *GROWS | WHEN YOU GET *POPULAR"
  },
  {
   "text": "So check the growth, not the speed on your laptop. Did you guess that big?",
   "caps": "CHECK THE *GROWTH | DID YOU GUESS *THAT BIG?"
  }
 ],
